# AI Forging — Project Conventions

> This project has been onboarded to the **AI Forging** framework. The full conventions library lives under `.aiforging/`. This file is a pointer for Claude (and for humans opening the repo) to the conventions that apply here.

## Prerequisites (important — read before working in this repo)

This project has been onboarded by `/aiforging:setup`, which wrote an `enabledPlugins` block into this repo's `.claude/settings.json`:

```json
{
  "enabledPlugins": {
    "superpowers@claude-plugins-official": true,
    "aiforging@claude-plugins-official": true
  }
}
```

That means **Claude Code will auto-activate `superpowers` and `aiforging` whenever it runs in this repo** — as long as those plugins are installed at the machine level. Plugins are installed once per user via `/plugin install` and then enabled per-scope via the settings file above, so anyone who clones this repo gets the same skill stack active for free, without having to edit their personal config.

AI Forging does NOT redistribute these plugins; they are peer dependencies. If Claude Code logs a warning that one of them isn't installed, install it at the machine level and re-open Claude:

1. **`superpowers`** (by Jesse Vincent — [github.com/obra/superpowers](https://github.com/obra/superpowers), accepted into the official Anthropic marketplace Jan 2026). Provides:
   - `superpowers:test-driven-development` — used for every Fire-stage test-first cycle in this repo.
   - `superpowers:brainstorming` — used for feature spec work.
   - `superpowers:writing-plans` — used for plan authoring.
   - `superpowers:executing-plans` — used for walking plans during implementation.
   - `superpowers:subagent-driven-development` — used by `hammer-refactor` to dispatch one subagent per refactor slice.

   Preferred install (matches the identifier written into `.claude/settings.json`): `/plugin install superpowers@claude-plugins-official`. Alternative: `/plugin marketplace add obra/superpowers` then `/plugin install superpowers@superpowers-dev` — but if you take that route you'll need to update `enabledPlugins` in `.claude/settings.json` to use `superpowers@superpowers-dev` instead.

2. **`aiforging`** — required for `/aiforging:setup`, `architecture-analyzer`, and future `/aiforging:update-targets`. Not strictly required to run the `hammer-refactor` skill on its own: a copy of that skill is committed at `.claude/skills/hammer-refactor/SKILL.md` inside this repo, which makes it discoverable to Claude Code even without the plugin installed. But enabling it via `enabledPlugins` lets you run maintenance commands from inside this repo.

**If `superpowers` is not installed at the machine level when working in this repo, Claude will be missing foundational skills** — even though `enabledPlugins` lists it. Install it first, then come back.

## The framework in one minute

- **Fire** — Every feature starts with a failing test. Delegate to `superpowers:test-driven-development`. Red, Green, Refactor. No implementation before a failing test exists.
- **Hammer** — After the test suite is green, run the post-TDD refactor pass by invoking the `hammer-refactor` skill (committed at `.claude/skills/hammer-refactor/SKILL.md`). The skill merges patterns from **two tiers** — the shared tier at the forge workspace level (filtered by this repo's detected stack) plus the target-local tier in this repo's `.aiforging/patterns/` and `.aiforging/anti-patterns/` — proposes a prioritized slice list, and dispatches one fresh-context subagent per approved slice via `superpowers:subagent-driven-development`. Human reviews each slice's diff before the next one dispatches.
- **Tempering** — Any new pattern or anti-pattern observed during real refactor work becomes its own `.md` file. The reactive **`capture-pattern` skill** (committed at `.claude/skills/capture-pattern/SKILL.md`) watches for corrective moments and asks whether the pattern should be **shared** (written to the workspace-level shared tier with `applies-to` frontmatter, available to all same-stack targets) or **target-local** (written to this repo's `.aiforging/patterns/` or `.aiforging/anti-patterns/`, specific to this repo). One pattern, one file. The library grows monotonically — we never edit old patterns silently. Every captured file is automatically picked up by the next `hammer-refactor` run.

## Architecture, in short

Follow the folder layout in `.aiforging/architecture/domain-driven-hexagonal.md`. The short version:

- `src/Domain/Feature/Subfeature/<Layer>/` where `<Layer>` is `Controller`, `Service`, `Repository`, `Entity`, `ValueObject`, or `DTO`.
- Tests mirror the `src/` structure in `tests/Domain/Feature/Subfeature/<Layer>Test/`.
- Controllers are single-action — one `__invoke` / `handle` / `execute` method per class. See `.aiforging/architecture/single-action-controllers.md`.
- Repositories are separate classes from Entities. Services depend on `XxxRepositoryInterface`, not the framework's EntityManager / DbContext / DataSource directly. See `.aiforging/architecture/repositories.md`.
- Domain concepts are Value Objects, not primitives. DTOs cross layer boundaries. See `.aiforging/architecture/dtos-and-value-objects.md`.
- Naming rules are enforced — see `.aiforging/architecture/naming.md`.

## Tests, in short

- Tests must be able to build the database schema from the entity graph, not from migration files. See `.aiforging/tdd/test-harness-requirements.md`.
- Repository tests hit a real isolated database. No mocked Repositories. See `.aiforging/tdd/repository-testing.md`.
- If the test harness cannot satisfy the capability contract, STOP and fix the harness before writing new Repository tests.

## Subagent orchestration, in short

When dispatching work to fresh-context subagents (via `superpowers:subagent-driven-development` or directly from a forge workspace plan), follow the rules in `.aiforging/subagent-orchestration/README.md`. The short version:

- The **parent conversation is a conductor**, not a coder. It reads `plan.md`, dispatches slices to subagents, and verifies each one — it never writes production code itself.
- Every subagent prompt **points at `plan.md`** rather than embedding slice details inline. This way, if the plan is updated mid-execution, the next subagent reads the current version.
- Every subagent **reads this repo's `CLAUDE.md`** (this file) before touching code, so it inherits the conventions.
- **Dispatch ordering matters.** Slices with explicit dependencies dispatch sequentially; independent slices may run in parallel if the plan allows it.
- **Every Fire sequence closes with a `[hammer]` slice** that dispatches `hammer-refactor` against the files the sequence touched.
- Subagents **check off their own tasks** in `plan.md`. The parent never fabricates a checkbox completion.

## What Claude should do in this repo

1. **First session in this repo**: read `.aiforging/ANALYSIS.md` if it exists — it's the architecture-analyzer's report on where this codebase stands relative to AI Forging ideals. Also check whether this repo is registered in a forge workspace — if so, the current cross-repo plan likely lives at `<forge-workspace>/docs/features/<feature-name>/plan.md`, not in this repo. Ask the user where their forge workspace is if it's not obvious from the session context; a forge workspace is a directory with a `CLAUDE.md` marker containing the string `AI Forging workspace` and a `docs/features/` tree, and the current repo's absolute path lives in that workspace's `.claude/settings.local.json` under `permissions.additionalDirectories`.
2. **Before writing code**: write the failing test. Use `superpowers:test-driven-development`.
3. **After green tests**: if a refactor pass is warranted, invoke the `hammer-refactor` skill. It reads patterns from both the workspace shared tier and this repo's target-local tier. Do NOT refactor ad-hoc without a pattern reference.
4. **After a Hammer pass, OR during any interactive code review**: if you observed a reusable rule that the pattern library doesn't already capture, follow the `capture-pattern` skill (at `.claude/skills/capture-pattern/SKILL.md`) to persist it. The skill asks whether the pattern should be shared (workspace-level, available to all same-stack targets) or target-local (this repo only). This is the Tempering pillar — the mechanism by which the library grows. The `capture-pattern` skill biases toward NOT prompting, so only offer when the correction clearly encodes a reusable, structural rule.
5. **Before merging**: wait for human review. Four gates — tests, code review, architecture decisions, deployment authorization.

## What Claude should NOT do in this repo

- **Never skip Fire.** No implementation before a failing test.
- **Never refactor without a pattern reference.** If a change doesn't correspond to a file in either tier (workspace shared `.aiforging/patterns/` or target-local `.aiforging/patterns/`), it's either out of scope or a candidate for a new pattern — which is a Tempering task, not a Hammer task.
- **Never collapse the pattern library into a single file.** One pattern, one file.
- **Never mock the database in a Repository test.** Use the real test harness.
- **Never introduce a fat controller.** See `.aiforging/anti-patterns/fat-controller.md`.
- **Never sprinkle primitives through the domain.** See `.aiforging/anti-patterns/primitive-obsession.md`.
- **Never autonomously deploy.** Human gates are not optional.

## Where the current feature plan lives

AI Forging plans are **centralized in the forge workspace**, not fragmented per-repo. If this repo is being worked on as part of a cross-repo feature, the current `plan.md` for that feature lives at `<forge-workspace>/docs/features/<feature-name>/plan.md`, and this repo will be named in the plan's `[target repo]` tags for each slice. Ask the user which feature is active if it's not obvious from context.

If someone is working directly in this repo (not from a forge workspace), they can still use `hammer-refactor` and the local pattern library — but cross-repo coordination will be missing. That's a conscious trade-off.

